> The difference between Guo Bao Rou and regular sweet and sour pork comes down to three things: paper-thin slices that fry shatteringly crisp, a lighter rice vinegar-forward sauce, and the characteristic streaming hot vegetables folded in at the end. The double-fry technique isn't about extra crispiness here—it's about cooking the pork through before the exterior burns.

== Slice and Marinate the Pork ==

Slice @pork loin{450%g} into very thin slices—about 5 mm (¼ inch) thick. This isn't thick-cut pork; the thinner the slice, the more shatteringly crispy it becomes after frying.

In a bowl, combine the pork slices with:
- @light soy sauce{15%ml}
- @salt{3%g}
- @Shaoxing wine{15%ml}
- @water{45%ml}

Work the marinade into each slice with your hands, ensuring full coverage. Cover and refrigerate for at least ~{30%minutes}.

== Prepare the Starch Paste ==

In a #medium bowl{}, dissolve @potato starch{230%g} in @water{360%ml}. Stir until completely dissolved and let rest for ~{15%minutes}.

The starch will settle to the bottom, leaving water on top. Slowly pour off the water, leaving behind a thick potato starch paste at the bottom of the bowl.

== Make the Sauce ==

In a separate bowl, whisk together until the sugar dissolves:
- @sugar{100%g}
- @rice vinegar{150%ml}
- @salt{3%g}
- @sesame oil{5%ml}

Set aside. The sugar should be fully dissolved before you start cooking—this ensures an even glaze on the pork.

== Coat the Pork ==

Add the marinated pork slices to the bowl with the potato starch paste. Use your hands to coat every piece thoroughly—the coating should be wet and somewhat gluey, not dry and powdery.

If the mixture seems too thick or dry to coat properly, add @water{15%ml} at a time, working it in until you achieve a consistency where each slice is evenly coated with a thin, wet layer of starch.

== Double-Fry the Pork ==

Pour @oil{500%ml} into a #deep pot{} or #wok{}. Heat slowly to 120-150°C (250-300°F)—no higher, or the pork will burn before cooking through.

Working in batches, carefully lower the pork slices one at a time into the oil, leaving space between each piece—they will stick together if overcrowded.

Fry each batch for ~{2%minutes} until the exterior is set but not yet golden. Transfer to a #wire rack{} to drain. The pork won't be fully cooked at this stage.

Once all the pork has been fried once, return the oil to 120-150°C (250-300°F). Fry the pork a second time in batches for ~{30%seconds} until deeply golden and crispy. Remove to the wire rack.

== Assemble the Dish ==

If using a wok, carefully pour the hot oil into a heat-proof container, leaving about @oil{15%ml} behind in the wok.

Place the wok over medium heat. Add @minced ginger{15%g} and @minced garlic{15%g}. Cook for ~{30%seconds} until fragrant.

Pour in the sauce mixture and turn the heat to high. Cook for ~{2-3%minutes}, reducing until you have about 80%ml (⅓ cup) of sauce remaining—it should be slightly syrupy.

Working quickly, add the streaming hot vegetables and fried pork:
- @carrot{½} (julienned into thin matchsticks)
- @scallions{4} (white parts only, julienned)
- @cilantro{15%g} (roughly chopped)

Toss everything together rapidly—the sauce should form a thin, glossy coating on each piece of pork. Serve immediately, before the coating loses its crunch.